      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Snacks or shoes?"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "You have 50 rupees: spend it on snacks or save for new shoes? You cannot do both. This is the basic problem of economics."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "actors",
          "command and mixed",
          "cost-benefit",
          "economics",
          "opportunity cost",
          "scarcity",
          "three questions",
          "traditional and market"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "Economics deals with how people choose to use limited resources to satisfy their wants.\nScarcity means resources are limited but wants are unlimited, so choices must be made.\nOpportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative given up when we make a choice.\nEvery society must answer: what to produce, how to produce it and for whom to produce.\nIn a traditional economy, customs decide. In a market economy, prices and private firms decide.\nIn a command economy the government plans production. A mixed economy, like India, combines market and government roles.\nHouseholds, firms and the government are the main actors in an economy.\nGood choices compare benefits with costs."
        }
      },
